answer:
Article 14 sets out the human oversight requirements for high-risk systems. Under Article 14(4), the people assigned oversight must be enabled to:
(a) properly understand the system’s capacities and limitations and monitor its operation, including detecting anomalies and unexpected performance;
(b) stay aware of the tendency to automatically rely or over-rely on its output (automation bias);
(c) correctly interpret the output;
(d) decide not to use the system, or to disregard, override or reverse its output;
(e) intervene in operation or stop the system through a “stop” button or similar.
Article 14(5) adds that, for Annex III point 1(a) systems, no decision based on an identification may be taken unless separately verified by at least two competent persons. That does not apply to your system.
Article 26(2) requires deployers to assign oversight to people with the necessary competence, training and authority, and support.
The text does not say how oversight should be organised day to day or what training content is required.

If the system is high-risk (the text does not decide that), Article 16 and Article 43 apply to the provider before placing it on the market. For an Annex III point 3(d) system, Article 43(2) requires the internal control procedure in Annex VI, which does not involve a notified body.
Before placing it on the market, the provider must, under Article 16:
ensure compliance with Chapter III, Section 2 (Articles 8 to 15);
have a quality management system (Article 17) and keep documentation (Article 18);
complete the conformity assessment (Article 43), draw up an EU declaration of conformity (Article 47), and affix the CE marking (Article 48);
register in the EU database (Article 49(1)).
The system itself must, under Article 12(1), technically allow automatic logging of events over its lifetime. Article 14(1) requires it to be designed so that natural persons can effectively oversee it, and Article 14(4) lists what overseers must be able to do. Article 15(1) requires an appropriate level of accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity, and Article 13(3)(b) requires the accuracy level and metrics to be stated in the instructions for use.
The text does not define what accuracy level is required, and it does not say how a monitoring tool should decide which behaviour is “prohibited.”
